Comparison of all three approaches
Feature	               Approach 1	                              Approach 2	                       Approach 3
Technique	           Sliding Window	                         Direct Comparison	                  Sliding Window
Data structure	          ArrayList	                                  None	                             HashMap
Window size                3  	                                       3                                    3
Time complexity	          O(26n)	                                  O(n)	                               O(n) average
Space complexity	       O(1)	                                      O(1)	                               O(1)
Beginner friendly	       Yes	                                      Very easy	                            Yes


class Solution {

    /*
    PROBLEM: COUNT GOOD SUBSTRINGS
    =============================

    APPROACH 1: DIRECT CHARACTER COMPARISON


    PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
    ======================

    We are given a string str.

    We need to count the number of substrings of length exactly 3
    in which all three characters are distinct.

    Example:

    str = "aababcabc"

    Substrings of length 3:

    "aab" -> Not good
    "aba" -> Not good
    "bab" -> Not good
    "aba" -> Not good
    "bac" -> Good
    "aca" -> Not good
    "cab" -> Good

    Answer = 2


    MAIN IDEA:
    ==========

    Since the required substring size is exactly 3,
    we do not need a frequency array or a HashMap.

    We can directly compare the three characters.

    Consider a substring:

    str[i-1], str[i], str[i+1]

    For all three characters to be distinct,
    the following three conditions must be satisfied:

    1. Current character != Previous character
    2. Current character != Next character
    3. Previous character != Next character

    If all three conditions are true,
    we have found a good substring.

    Therefore, increase ans.


    WHY DO WE START i FROM 1?
    =========================

    We are accessing three characters:

    Previous character = str[i-1]
    Current character  = str[i]
    Next character     = str[i+1]

    If i starts from 0, str[i-1] will be invalid.

    Therefore, we start i from 1.

    Also, we need i+1 to be within the string.

    The last valid value of i is n-2.

    Therefore, the loop runs from:

    i = 1 to i <= n-2


    ALGORITHM:
    ==========

    STEP 1:
    Calculate the length of the string.

    STEP 2:
    Initialize ans = 0.

    STEP 3:
    Traverse the string using a for loop from i = 1 to n-2.

    STEP 4:
    Store the three characters:
    - prev = str[i-1]
    - curr = str[i]
    - next = str[i+1]

    STEP 5:
    Check all three pairwise comparisons:

    curr != prev
    curr != next
    prev != next

    STEP 6:
    If all conditions are true, increment ans.

    STEP 7:
    Return ans.


    TIME COMPLEXITY:
    ================

    O(n)

    We traverse the string once.

    Each iteration performs only three character comparisons,
    which take constant time.


    AUXILIARY SPACE:
    ================

    O(1)

    We use only a few variables.

    No extra data structure is required.
    */


    public int countGoodSubstrings(String str) {

        int n = str.length(); // Length of the string

        int ans = 0; // Stores the total number of good substrings

        // Traverse the string.
        // We start from index 1 because we need a previous character.
        // We stop at n-2 because we also need a next character.
        for(int i = 1; i <= n-2; i++) {

            // Store the current character.
            char curr = str.charAt(i);

            // Store the previous character.
            char prev = str.charAt(i-1);

            // Store the next character.
            char next = str.charAt(i+1);

            // Check whether all three characters are different.
            //
            // curr != prev -> Current and previous are different.
            // curr != next -> Current and next are different.
            // prev != next -> Previous and next are different.
            //
            // All three conditions must be true.
            if(curr != prev && curr != next && prev != next) {

                // All three characters are distinct.
                // Therefore, we found a good substring.
                ans++;
            }
        }

        // Return the total number of good substrings.
        return ans;
    }
}
class Solution {

    /*
    PROBLEM: COUNT GOOD SUBSTRINGS
    =============================

    APPROACH 2: FIXED SIZE SLIDING WINDOW + FREQUENCY ARRAYLIST


    PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
    ======================

    We are given a string str.

    A good substring is a substring of length exactly 3
    in which all three characters are distinct.

    Distinct means all characters must be different.

    Example:
    str = "aababcabc"

    Substrings of length 3:

    "aab" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "aba" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "bab" -> Not good (b is repeated)
    "aba" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "bac" -> Good (all characters are different)
    "aca" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "cab" -> Good (all characters are different)

    Answer = 2


    MAIN IDEA:
    ==========

    We use a fixed-size sliding window of size 3.

    We create an ArrayList of size 26 to store the
    frequency of all lowercase English alphabets.

    Initially, all frequencies are zero.

    Index mapping:

    'a' - 'a' = 0
    'b' - 'a' = 1
    'c' - 'a' = 2
    ...
    'z' - 'a' = 25

    Whenever a character enters the window:
    - Calculate its index using ch - 'a'.
    - Increase its frequency in the ArrayList.

    Whenever the window size becomes 3:
    - Traverse the frequency ArrayList.
    - If any character has frequency greater than 1,
      the substring is not good.
    - Otherwise, the substring is good.
    - Remove the leftmost character from the window.
    - Decrease its frequency.
    - Move i forward.


    WHY DO WE CHECK FREQUENCY > 1?
    ==============================

    Our window contains exactly 3 characters.

    If any character has frequency greater than 1,
    it means at least one character is repeated.

    Therefore, all three characters are not distinct.

    If no character has frequency greater than 1,
    all three characters must be different.


    ALGORITHM:
    ==========

    STEP 1:
    Initialize i and j at index 0.

    STEP 2:
    Create an ArrayList of size 26 with all values initialized
    to zero.

    STEP 3:
    Traverse the string using pointer j.

    STEP 4:
    Calculate the index of the current character:

    idx = str.charAt(j) - 'a'

    Increase its frequency in the ArrayList.

    STEP 5:
    When the window size becomes 3:
    - Initialize flag = true.
    - Traverse all 26 positions of the ArrayList.
    - If any frequency is greater than 1, make flag = false.
    - If flag is true, increment ans.

    STEP 6:
    Remove the leftmost character from the window.
    Decrease its frequency in the ArrayList.
    Move i forward.

    STEP 7:
    Move j forward and continue.

    STEP 8:
    Return ans.


    TIME COMPLEXITY:
    ================

    O(26 * n)

    - We traverse the string once: O(n).
    - For every complete window, we traverse 26 positions:
      O(26).

    Total = O(26 * n).

    Since 26 is constant, overall complexity is O(n).


    AUXILIARY SPACE:
    ================

    O(26) = O(1)

    We use an ArrayList of fixed size 26.
    */


    public int countGoodSubstrings(String str) {

        int n = str.length(); // Length of the string

        int ans = 0; // Stores the total number of good substrings

        // Create an ArrayList of size 26.
        // Initially, the frequency of every character is zero.
        ArrayList<Integer> arr = new ArrayList<>(Collections.nCopies(26, 0));

        int i = 0; // Starting index of the window
        int j = 0; // Ending index of the window

        // Traverse the string using pointer j.
        while(j < n) {

            // Current character entering the window.
            char ch = str.charAt(j);

            // Convert the character into an index.
            // Example: 'c' - 'a' = 2
            int idx = ch - 'a';

            // Increase the frequency of the current character.
            arr.set(idx, arr.get(idx) + 1);

            // Check whether the current window size has become 3.
            if(j-i+1 == 3) {

                // Assume that the current substring is good.
                Boolean flag = true;

                // Traverse all 26 characters to check their frequencies.
                for(int a = 0; a < 26; a++) {

                    // If any character appears more than once,
                    // the current substring is not good.
                    if(arr.get(a) > 1) {

                        flag = false;
                    }
                }

                // If no character is repeated,
                // all three characters are distinct.
                if(flag == true) {

                    // Increase the count of good substrings.
                    ans++;
                }

                // Store the leftmost character before removing it.
                char left = str.charAt(i);

                // Move the starting pointer forward.
                i++;

                // Calculate the index of the removed character.
                int index = left - 'a';

                // Decrease its frequency because it is
                // no longer part of the current window.
                arr.set(index, arr.get(index) - 1);
            }

            // Move the ending pointer forward.
            j++;
        }

        // Return the total number of good substrings.
        return ans;
    }
}
class Solution {

    /*
    PROBLEM: COUNT GOOD SUBSTRINGS
    =============================

    APPROACH 3: FIXED SIZE SLIDING WINDOW + HASHMAP


    PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
    ======================

    We are given a string str.

    A good substring is a substring of length exactly 3
    in which all three characters are distinct.

    Distinct means all characters must be different.

    Example:

    str = "aababcabc"

    Substrings of length 3:

    "aab" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "aba" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "bab" -> Not good (b is repeated)
    "aba" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "bac" -> Good (all characters are different)
    "aca" -> Not good (a is repeated)
    "cab" -> Good (all characters are different)

    Answer = 2


    MAIN IDEA:
    ==========

    We use a fixed-size sliding window of size 3.

    We maintain a HashMap to store the frequency of
    characters present in the current window.

    Key   = Character
    Value = Frequency of that character

    Example:

    Current window = "abc"

    HashMap:

    a -> 1
    b -> 1
    c -> 1

    Map size = 3

    Since the window contains exactly 3 distinct characters,
    it is a good substring.

    Therefore:

    If map.size() == 3, increment ans.


    WHY DO WE USE A HASHMAP?
    ========================

    A HashMap stores characters along with their frequencies.

    Whenever a character enters the window:
    - Increase its frequency in the HashMap.

    Whenever a character leaves the window:
    - Decrease its frequency.
    - If its frequency becomes zero, remove it from the map.

    IMPORTANT:

    We must remove a character from the map when its frequency
    becomes zero.

    Otherwise, the map will contain characters that are no
    longer present in the current window.

    Since our window size is exactly 3, the map can contain
    at most 3 distinct characters.


    WHY DOES map.size() == 3 WORK?
    ==============================

    Our current window always contains exactly 3 characters.

    Consider these cases:

    Window = "aab"

    HashMap:
    a -> 2
    b -> 1

    map.size() = 2

    Therefore, it is NOT a good substring.

    --------------------------------

    Window = "abc"

    HashMap:
    a -> 1
    b -> 1
    c -> 1

    map.size() = 3

    Therefore, it IS a good substring.

    --------------------------------

    Since the window contains exactly 3 characters,
    having 3 distinct keys in the map guarantees that
    all three characters are different.


    SLIDING WINDOW VARIABLES:
    ========================

    i:
    Starting index of the current window.

    j:
    Ending index of the current window.

    ans:
    Stores the total number of good substrings.

    m1:
    HashMap that stores the frequency of characters
    present in the current window.


    ALGORITHM:
    ==========

    STEP 1:
    Calculate the length of the string.

    STEP 2:
    Initialize two pointers i and j at index 0.

    STEP 3:
    Create a HashMap to store character frequencies.

    STEP 4:
    Traverse the string using pointer j.

    STEP 5:
    Add the current character str[j] to the HashMap
    and increase its frequency.

    STEP 6:
    Check whether the current window size becomes 3.

    If the window size becomes 3:

    - Check whether map.size() == 3.
    - If yes, increment ans because all three characters
      are distinct.

    STEP 7:
    Remove the leftmost character from the current window.

    - Store str[i] in left.
    - Move i forward.
    - Decrease the frequency of left in the HashMap.
    - If its frequency becomes zero, remove it from the map.

    STEP 8:
    Move j forward and continue.

    STEP 9:
    Return ans.


    TIME COMPLEXITY:
    ================

    O(n) average time.

    - We traverse the string once.
    - Each character is inserted into the HashMap once.
    - Each character is removed from the HashMap at most once.
    - HashMap insertion, deletion and lookup take O(1)
      average time.

    Overall = O(n).


    AUXILIARY SPACE:
    ================

    O(1)

    Since the window size is fixed at 3,
    the HashMap can contain at most 3 distinct characters.

    Therefore, it requires constant space.
    */


    public int countGoodSubstrings(String str) {

        int n = str.length(); // Length of the given string

        int i = 0; // Starting index of the window
        int j = 0; // Ending index of the window

        int ans = 0; // Stores the total number of good substrings

        // Create a HashMap to store character frequencies.
        //
        // Key   = Character
        // Value = Frequency of that character
        //
        // Example:
        // a -> 2
        // b -> 1
        HashMap<Character, Integer> m1 = new HashMap<>();


        // Traverse the string using pointer j.
        while(j < n) {

            // Current character entering the window.
            char ch = str.charAt(j);

            // Increase the frequency of the current character.
            //
            // If the character is already present,
            // its frequency will increase by 1.
            //
            // If the character is not present,
            // a new entry will be created with frequency 1.
            m1.put(ch, m1.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);


            // Check whether the current window size has become 3.
            if(j - i + 1 == 3) {

                // If map size is 3, it means there are
                // exactly 3 distinct characters in the window.
                //
                // Since the window itself contains 3 characters,
                // all three characters must be different.
                if(m1.size() == 3) {

                    // We found a good substring.
                    // Increase the answer.
                    ans++;
                }


                // Store the leftmost character of the window.
                // This is the character that we are going to remove.
                char left = str.charAt(i);


                // Move the starting pointer forward.
                //
                // This removes the leftmost character
                // from the current window.
                i++;


                // Decrease the frequency of the removed character.
                m1.put(left, m1.get(left) - 1);


                // Check whether the frequency of the removed
                // character has become zero.
                if(m1.get(left) == 0) {

                    // Remove the character completely from the map.
                    //
                    // We do this because this character is no longer
                    // present in the current sliding window.
                    m1.remove(left);
                }
            }


            // Move the ending pointer forward.
            // The next character will enter the window.
            j++;
        }


        // Return the total number of good substrings.
        return ans;
    }
}
